# converted from 05-the-orders-choice.md
@title The Order's Choice
@bg mire
@mood dread

narrate The Order declared the Warden a deserter on the ninth day, and the wording was careful, and the wording was the worst part of it.
corin neutral acted outside the framework of the quarantine mandate in a manner inconsistent with his commission, in the company of an unlicensed bearer of a Class One prohibition.
narrate They did not say he had betrayed them. They said he had
narrate Class One prohibition. That was the mark. There were three of them in the archive and Corin was the fourth.
narrate And then — in the same dispatch, on the same page, in the same careful language — the Order did the thing that Corin had been waiting five years to see.
narrate They requisitioned the Mire.
narrate It was there in black and white. The eastern quarantine zone, being no longer administrable by the existing framework, is hereby transferred to the direct authority of the Office of the Warden, to be held, managed, and if necessary cleared.
narrate Nine thousand people in Kestrel had been saved four days earlier and the Order had written a single line about it in an appendix. Forty thousand people in the Mire were a resource now — an asset to be managed, a border to be drawn, a problem that had finally become worth owning, because the only man who could end the Fever was standing in it.
narrate The Warden read it twice.
narrate Then he folded it very carefully, the way he folded everything, and put it in his coat, and said:
cael neutral They'll offer you a title.
corin neutral What?
cael neutral They will offer you a title, and a commission, and a house in Ashford, and they will do it within the month.
cael neutral And I want you to understand exactly why, because you are twenty-four and you have a condition, and you will not see it coming.
corin neutral Tell me.
cael neutral Because the Fever is the only thing that has ever given them power,
cael neutral It is the whole of their mandate. Every road they seal, every village they burn, every ledger I have ever written — it exists because there is a Fever. And you are the man who can end it.
narrate He looked east.
corin neutral They do not want you to end it. They want you to hold it. They want a boy with a mark in his hand who can be pointed at a city and told not yet.
narrate They walked east for two days.
narrate Corin did not sleep much. Bryn walked on his left. The Warden wrote everything down, and on the second evening he read some of it back, because that was the arrangement, and because Corin had asked him to.
narrate The Warden closed the ledger.
cael neutral You should stop asking me questions,
cael neutral Every answer I give you is a memory you no longer have, and I am beginning to think that reading them back is not the mercy I thought it was.
corin neutral No,
corin neutral Keep doing it. If I can't feel it, I want to know it.
cael neutral That is a very strange thing to want.
corin neutral It's the only thing I've got left,
corin neutral Somebody has to know who I was.
narrate They came into the Mire on the third morning.
narrate And on the fourth morning, at a place where the ground stopped pretending, there was an old man sitting on a stone with a cup in his hands, waiting.
narrate He was very thin. Thinner than the ledger said. His coat had been mended in four places and then in eleven more, and there was a mark on his palm that had gone wrong — not a circle with a hand inside it, but a shape that had cracked, the black gone grey and spidered out across the whole of his hand and up his wrist.
narrate He looked up when they came out of the trees.
cael neutral Ah,
cael neutral There you are.
narrate Corin did not know him.
narrate That was the first thing. He stood at the edge of a clearing in a country he had spent a year in and could not remember, and looked at an old man on a stone, and felt nothing at all.
corin neutral I'm sorry,
corin neutral Have we met?
narrate And Cael — who had spent twelve years learning not to feel anything, and five years walking north, and one morning sitting on a stone waiting for a boy he had arranged like a garden — looked at him for a long moment.
narrate And then he laughed.
narrate It was a short, cracked, astonished sound, and it was the second time in his life that Corin had made him do it, and Corin would not remember either.
cael neutral You asked me that already,
cael neutral Once. You asked me for her voice and then you asked me to sit down.
corin neutral ...I did?
cael neutral You did.
cael neutral You don't have it anymore. The field. The tea. Any of it.
narrate Cael stood up, slowly, because standing up had become a thing that required planning
corin neutral No.
cael neutral Good,
cael neutral Then I'll tell you. It's a long story and I've got perhaps four days and you've got a ledger, so sit down and get your man with the book ready.
